# Project Structure Standards

> file_patterns: ["*.py", "**/*.py"]
> description: Guidelines for Python project organization focusing on maintainability and AI-friendly structure

## File Organization Principles
> required: true

### File Size and Responsibility
- Keep files small (recommended < 200 lines)
- One primary functionality per file
- Split large files into focused modules
- Use clear, purpose-indicating filenames

### Function-First Approach
- Prefer functions over classes
- Use pure functions when possible
- Keep functions focused and small
- Group related functions logically

### Class Usage Guidelines
- Use classes only when necessary:
  - For data containers (dataclasses)
  - When state management is essential
  - For framework requirements
  - For clear interface definitions
- Keep classes small and focused
- Prefer composition over inheritance

## Directory Structure
> required: true

### Directory Size Management
- Keep directories focused and small
- Limit files per directory (recommended < 10)
- Create subdirectories for logical grouping
- Maintain shallow but wide directory structure

### Directory Naming and Organization
- Use descriptive directory names
- Group by functionality not type
- Create dedicated feature directories
- Keep test directories parallel to source

### Example Structure
```
project/
├── core/
│   ├── config/
│   │   ├── settings.py
│   │   └── constants.py
│   └── utils/
│       ├── file_handlers.py
│       └── validators.py
├── features/
│   ├── auth/
│   │   ├── login.py
│   │   └── permissions.py
│   └── data_processing/
│       ├── cleaners.py
│       └── transformers.py
└── tests/
    ├── core/
    └── features/
```

## Module Organization

### Import Management
- Keep imports at file top
- Group imports logically
- Use absolute imports
- Minimize dependencies between modules

### Function Organization
- Place main functionality first
- Group helper functions near related code
- Keep utility functions in separate modules
- Use meaningful function names

## Best Practices

### Code Organization
- Prefer flat structures over deep nesting
- Keep code paths simple and traceable
- Use meaningful file and directory names
- Maintain consistent organization patterns

### Functional Programming
- Use immutable data when possible
- Avoid global state
- Implement pure functions
- Use list comprehensions and generators
- Leverage functional tools (map, filter, reduce)

### Context Management
- Keep context size manageable for AI
- Organize related functionality together
- Make dependencies explicit
- Document file relationships

### File Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with underscores
- Make names descriptive but concise
- Include purpose in name
- Follow consistent patterns:
  - `process_data.py` not `processor.py`
  - `validate_input.py` not `validation.py`
  - `transform_json.py` not `json_handler.py`

## AI-Friendly Organization

### Context Optimization
- Keep related code easily discoverable
- Maintain clear file boundaries
- Use consistent patterns
- Document relationships between files

### Directory Navigation
- Create logical groupings
- Use clear naming patterns
- Maintain reasonable directory sizes
- Document directory purposes

### Code Discoverability
- Use descriptive names
- Maintain consistent structure
- Keep dependencies clear
- Document code relationships

## Validation Requirements
- Files must be focused and small
- Directories must be well-organized
- Functions should be preferred over classes
- Structure must be AI-friendly
- Dependencies must be clear and minimal
- Documentation must explain organization
- Names must be clear and purposeful

## Directory Organization
Standards for directory structure:
- Separate source code, tests, docs, and configs
- Use README.md for functionality documentation
- Store architecture diagrams in diagrams/
- Avoid excessive directory nesting

## Dependency Management
Guidelines for managing project dependencies:
- Use venv for virtual environment management (.venv directory)
- Use .env for configuration and secrets
- Use .gitignore for version control management
- Follow dependency injection principles

## Project Layout

### Required Files
> required: true

- README.md
- requirements.txt
- .env.example
- .gitignore

### Required Directories
> required: true

- src/
- tests/
- docs/
- config/

## Configuration Management
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Keep secrets separate from code
- Document all configuration options
- Provide example configurations

## Version Control
- Use meaningful .gitignore rules
- Keep sensitive data out of version control
- Document branching strategy
- Include contribution guidelines 